WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.257 --> 00:00:03.424
(upbeat hiphop music)

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.740
- So, welcome to another
episode of the "Addy Hour".

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.360
Today, I'm honored to be
able to host co-founder

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.730
of March for Our Lives, David Hogg.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.060
And today we're gonna be
jumping into a conversation

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.550
really talking about activism,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.530
self care, and ending gun violence.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.231
Now, I know for those
of you in the audience,

00:00:22.231 --> 00:00:23.930
a lot of you are probably
familiar with David,

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.370
you're familiar with his
work and his efforts.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:27.270
And I think today it will be nice

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.370
to really peel back a
little bit and talk about

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:31.870
some of the things that
he's been involved in,

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.940
some of his goals, but
also about him as a person,

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.480
and some of the things that
he's really trying to elevate

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.950
in terms of really
making sure that the work

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.230
that others are doing on the ground

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.070
in a lot of ways is also
highlighted as well.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.770
So, really excited to jump
into this conversation.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.960
Obviously, David is someone
who is thrust into activism

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.120
following the tragic
and unfortunate shooting

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.620
at his high school in
Parkland, Florida in 2018.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.900
And from that event, he and his
classmates have really vowed

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.090
to be change makers in a lot of ways.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.070
And he's resolved that
no other young person

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:07.270
should have to experience the
tragic impact of violence,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.130
of gun violence in particular.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:09.963
So, as I mentioned,

00:01:09.963 --> 00:01:11.820
he's the co-founder of
March for Our Lives.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.990
They've been involved in
lots of different initiatives

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.400
over the years.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.640
Just to highlight a few;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.630
the march they had in Washington, DC,

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.960
where he spoke to an
estimated 800,000 protesters.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.140
He and his sister,
Lauren are also authors,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.750
they co-authored "Never again",

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.530
which became a New York
Times' bestselling book.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:30.787
They also contributed to the book,

00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:33.950
"Glimmer of Hope: How
Tragedy Sparked a Movement."

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.540
And they'd been involved in
lots of different efforts

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.290
over the years, touring
across the country,

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.230
really meeting families and individuals,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.130
who've had losses at the
hands of gun violence,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.420
and just also educating themselves

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.700
about ways to really make an impact.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.580
David's also someone

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.170
who has a pretty large
social media following,

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.730
and he really uses his platform

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:55.130
to promote civic engagement,
activism, and voting.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:56.910
So again, just grateful to have David here

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.500
on the "Addy Hour" episode today.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.340
David, thanks so much for
taking the time to join us.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.600
- Yeah, thanks for having me on.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.240
- Of course.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.560
So again, a lot of people know about you,

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.670
maybe not know a lot about
you on the personal level,

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:12.300
but one of the things
that I really like to do

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.110
on this program too, is always
just to check in with folks.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.580
I think is important to remind people

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:17.950
that you're also a student.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.910
So, in the middle of all
this work that you're doing,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:21.220
you've also been taking classes,

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.930
you're coming to the end of the semester.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.030
So, I actually just wanted to start out

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.370
and see how things are with you these days

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.220
in the midst of everything
that's going on in the world,

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.420
you know, as you're
wrapping up the semester,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.120
just in your daily life, how
are you doing these days?

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.613
- Yeah, I would say I'm pretty good.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.963
Just trying to figure out,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.280
I dunno, it's kinda weird,

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.033
'cause when you take time
off of school or work,

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:49.350
I think a lot of people have
this feeling where it's like,

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.650
you don't have things to be
stressed out about anymore,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.540
but you still have this
stress for some reason,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.373
you know?
- Hm.

00:02:55.373 --> 00:02:56.900
- So I've been dealing with that.

00:02:58.058 --> 00:02:59.470
And I've just been thinking a lot more

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:01.370
about what I want to do in the future,

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:04.078
just because I'm now halfway

00:03:04.078 --> 00:03:08.600
through my four years of college,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.600
and thinking about different
opportunities and stuff,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.410
internships and things to do.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.280
Even though I really, honestly,

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.030
I wish I could just take time
off and like not do anything

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.520
for a minute because it's...

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.160
I think my generation, especially,

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.970
but pretty much Americans in general

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:31.200
have an arguably unhealthy obsession

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.580
with doing as much as possible,

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.180
and don't really value spending
time with friends and family

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.560
as much as I think we should,
'cause we think in a very,

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.230
almost like hyper capitalistic manner

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:46.780
that can really kinda hurt
a lot of our friendships,

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:50.253
relationships, and our
family relationships overall.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.360
Although, I think a lot
of us have been able

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.570
to spend more time this year

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.180
with our families and everything,

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.790
for some of us perhaps too much.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:59.880
(Addy laughs)

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.590
I think I'm doing pretty good overall,

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.690
just trying to be more accepting

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.740
of not necessarily knowing what
I'm gonna do in the future,

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.210
even though I know it'd be
something vaguely political,

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.883
or communications or whatever.

00:04:13.016 --> 00:04:17.270
Being more accepting of the
unknown and kind of embracing it

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:18.720
and taking care of myself
while I'm doing that.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.260
- Right, right, well, I
appreciate your honesty in that.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.340
And I think that's really
healthy in a lot of ways too.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.880
But I think it's good that
you're being honest with yourself

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.380
and also highlighting the tension

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.590
between all the work
that needs to be done,

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.510
but then also knowing that,
it's still good to slow down,

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:34.880
and spend time with family and friends,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.010
but there's not always an easy answer.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.107
So I think, just even the fact
that you're verbalizing it.

00:04:39.107 --> 00:04:40.930
So, I think for a lot of
us, sometimes it's easy,

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:41.780
like you said, not to verbalize it,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.600
and just go kind of 1000 miles an hour,

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.380
and now even taking
that time to pull back.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.470
I mean, on that note
too, you all have been,

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.960
I mean, I think, part of it is just

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.200
because you all have been so...

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.850
It isn't based in the
culture, like you said,

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.260
but all the important work
you all have been doing

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:57.420
over the last three years.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.400
So, just to kinda pull back a little bit,

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.030
what's that been like would you say

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:02.700
for the last few years being David Hogg.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.100
I know that sounds like a cheesy question,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.060
but I really just wanted
to kinda get a sense,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.173
or a capturing of what that
journey has been like for you.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.670
- I mean, it's certainly
had its ups and its downs.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.360
I would say that it's been really amazing

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.860
to see how my friends and I are able

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.490
to take an idea like doing
a March on Washington,

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.980
or talking about youth voting,

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.020
and really help turn it into reality.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.690
Although, we were by no
means the only people

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.890
that talked about youth
voting, for example.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.730
It's been incredible to see
the achievements we've had

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.420
even in the past three years,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.870
with one of the highest
youth voter turnouts in 2018,

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:42.270
on any non-presidential midterm.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.500
And really the highest youth voter turnout

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.433
in American history in 2020,
it's pretty remarkable.

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:56.610
So I think those are some of high points.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.380
I think some of the lower ones though are,

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.070
as I kinda talked about earlier
that on unhealthy obsession,

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.500
like hyper capitalistic
obsession with productivity

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.950
and like, that being the
only thing that matters,

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.920
paired with the trauma
and survivor's guilt

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.650
of going through shooting, and
talking to all these people

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:17.110
that have experienced gun
violence on a daily basis,

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.010
literally, probably, tens
of thousands of people

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:19.843
at this point.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:21.910
The tearing of those two things

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:23.380
is a really dangerous combination

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:25.790
that I've seen hurt a lot of people

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.493
that I know that have worked in,

00:06:28.377 --> 00:06:31.273
what I would call the
peace advocacy space.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.850
Because the work that
we do is very important,

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:38.440
but sometimes because it is so important,

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.150
we lose sight of how important we are

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:43.130
and the importance of taking
care of ourselves in this,

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:44.687
because of that guilt,
because of that PTSD,

00:06:44.687 --> 00:06:47.693
and that trauma, that
trauma-driven response.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:53.960
I think I've certainly had
my own challenges with that.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:57.260
Not just because of like notoriety,

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:58.970
or attention, or whatever.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.700
Much of which, quite frankly,

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:04.630
is because Parkland is a
predominantly white community

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.113
that's more affluent than
the average community.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:13.487
But it's also because
I share a lot of that

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:17.620
unhealthy obsession with productivity

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.640
and I for the longest time,
I just really liked it,

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.120
the work that we were
doing, like a campaign,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.510
I thought in 2018 we're just
gonna work 36 hours a day,

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:31.510
basically, every single day
and not stop for anything.

00:07:31.766 --> 00:07:33.330
I mean, literally, work oftentimes

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.630
until we got physically sick

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:36.870
to the point that we
couldn't work anymore,

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:38.080
which happened multiple times to me,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.580
and I know happened to
others that we worked with.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.850
But it's really taken
me broadening that view

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:47.700
and realizing that my self-care
and hanging out with friends

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.470
and talking to people, isn't
something to feel guilty about.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.230
It's something that's a necessity.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:54.960
It's part of my productivity.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.640
Cause this is not like
a campaign where it's

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.070
a sprint, you have November 3rd

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:03.270
or whatever day it is that
you're gonna finish by.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.140
We don't know when gun
violence is gonna end.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:08.590
It's much more accustomed
to an ultra marathon

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:10.530
where we don't know the
ultimate destination

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.770
and there's a lot of wrong
paths we can go down.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:14.020
So we really have to pace ourselves

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:16.100
and be even prepared to pass on the Baton

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.140
to the next generation,

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:18.813
although, I hope that's not necessary.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.910
So it's been a lot over
the past three years,

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:25.070
it's felt like a lifetime.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:28.270
So much travel, so many
people that I've met,

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:33.270
so many experiences, and I
really had to challenge myself

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.340
to figure out how to prioritize myself

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.730
and kinda putting on my oxygen mask first,

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:40.820
before I can help anyone else.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:43.155
And it's difficult when
we have this obsession

00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:46.650
with constant achievement
and constantly doing

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.000
more, and more, and more, and more,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.140
and being obsessed with productivity

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.767
instead of realizing the importance of,

00:08:53.767 --> 00:08:56.840
and it sounds super cliche,
and I kinda hate the term,

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.810
but like taking care of yourself.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:08:59.782
'Cause we hear it all the time

00:08:59.782 --> 00:09:01.380
but I never really
understood what that meant

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.073
until the past year or two.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:06.362
But because of that, I've really been able

00:09:06.362 --> 00:09:08.620
to take care of myself
and to take a step back

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.840
and think about how do
I wanna enter this space

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.470
instead of what are all the
holes that I need to fill in.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:16.330
So that's kind of what the
past three years had been like.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:19.910
I actually struggled a lot with PTSD,

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.610
not just from the shooting,
but actually from activism

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.475
in the threats and the harassment

00:09:27.475 --> 00:09:31.320
in even how hard we were working at times;

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.110
like getting four hours
sleep oftentimes, you know,

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:38.270
every day for a month or two
is not, it's not healthy.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:39.920
Especially, when you're a younger person

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:41.463
and you need a lot of sleep.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:46.170
And then you have to go in
front of people and talk,

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.170
and then you have to go
on the news and stuff.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:51.184
And you're concerned that, are
you taking up too much space,

00:09:51.184 --> 00:09:53.750
how do you uplift people properly,

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:55.090
but not in a tokenistic banner,

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.350
but how do you like still highlight

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:57.700
the issues that's going on?

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:03.330
And, luckily because, you know,

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.275
going to therapy for basically
the past year and a half,

00:10:05.275 --> 00:10:07.500
I've really been able to
start focusing much more

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.050
on taking care of myself and consistently,

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.150
checking my bases every day
of like, it seems ridiculous,

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.765
but just eating, drinking,
getting enough sleep,

00:10:15.765 --> 00:10:18.496
and focusing on those basic core things,

00:10:18.496 --> 00:10:20.960
it's all I can really start
to build that, you know,

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:24.010
on top of that foundation
to become more productive,

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.010
but in a healthy manner.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.753
- Yeah, yeah, I think that's so important.

00:10:27.753 --> 00:10:29.850
I mean, I think there's a
lot of maturity there too

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.210
and growth along the way.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:33.840
And that, not to sound that you
weren't mature early on too,

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.080
but just even the way that
you kind of characterize

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:38.010
like you were going a
thousand miles an hour

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.250
at the beginning, then you're realizing

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:40.480
where you need to pull back,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:41.920
where you need to have that self care.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:44.460
But, I imagine that's still not easy,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.840
because in some ways I think
that's what beats a cycle.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.970
'Cause you know, the less
sleep, the more work you do,

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:51.605
the more outcome, and the
more output you can see.

00:10:51.605 --> 00:10:53.960
So I'm curious, like how
did you move along that?

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:55.150
'Cause in some ways it's almost like

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:57.473
you had to sacrifice some of the work

00:10:57.473 --> 00:10:59.030
to be able to take care of yourself.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:00.987
And you talked about having
that long-term trajectory.

00:11:00.987 --> 00:11:03.397
I'd imagine that's still a
tension 'cause it's like,

00:11:03.397 --> 00:11:05.890
"Well, I'm not doing this
work, I'm pulling back.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.210
So X, Y, and Z is not getting done."

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:11.680
So how have you reconciled
that in your mind?

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.240
- I think the main way
I would've reconciled it

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:14.330
is realizing that

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:19.310
I think, 'cause we have a
very individualistic culture,

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.340
we have a tendency to
get very self-absorbed

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:22.400
in the work that we do and think that

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.693
we're the only person doing the work.

00:11:24.693 --> 00:11:26.600
I think that applies
to all of us, honestly,

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:29.680
because we're all the stars
of our own reality, TV shows,

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:31.063
in some sense, in our mind.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:35.460
I think what it took me realizing was

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:37.560
it was truly effectively delegating

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:41.410
and working to hire people
that were actually qualified

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:43.040
to do this work, had
experience doing this work

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:44.273
in the nonprofit space,

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.930
and organizing stuff to help do the work

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:49.820
within March for Our Lives,

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:51.943
or the organization is basically grown,

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:57.900
over like basically eight
times, over the past two years

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:59.560
in terms of our total number of staff.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:01.970
'Cause we were woefully understaffed,

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.880
and had a severe lack
of experience, honestly,

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:07.570
with a lot of the work that we are doing

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:08.510
and it's still youth-led,

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.830
but we have people that
have more experience,

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:12.646
helping execute the
plans that young people

00:12:12.646 --> 00:12:14.853
ultimately come up with
in the first place.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.450
So that's been the most
effective thing is like

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:19.710
hiring other people to do the work

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:21.940
that I didn't necessarily
need to be doing myself,

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:24.243
but I just found myself
doing a lot of the time,

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:29.420
and then just preoccupying
myself with school, 'cause it's,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:31.470
as you can imagine, going go into college

00:12:33.107 --> 00:12:38.107
and traveling every weekend
and going on like it's just,

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.110
I kind of had to realize that
there some sacrifices involved

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:42.039
in order for me to prioritize

00:12:42.039 --> 00:12:43.660
really what's best for my future,

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.860
and that's going to
college instead of just,

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:50.610
necessarily doing a ton of
work every day in this field

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.840
that in some ways it's
also just exhausting.

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:56.067
It's activism and a lot of the times

00:12:56.067 --> 00:13:01.067
it's a really thankless
job and it's difficult

00:13:01.773 --> 00:13:04.390
because you can believe that,

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:05.610
even if you do achieve these things

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.116
that somehow it's gonna like make up

00:13:07.116 --> 00:13:08.400
for that lack of self care,

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:10.113
but it doesn't, it really doesn't.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:14.880
So yeah, it's been difficult
making those decisions,

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:16.024
but you kinda have to
learn how to say, no,

00:13:16.024 --> 00:13:19.799
a lot of the time, even though it's hard

00:13:19.799 --> 00:13:23.210
and it feels like you're, you
know, it almost feels like...

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.160
'Cause we're told so often
about how important it is

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.730
to take every opportunity,

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:28.500
it kinda feels like you're
cutting off your right arm,

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:29.800
for lack of a better word.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:32.960
But you have to realize that,

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.060
even if it does feel that way,

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.940
ultimately, you have to be able to survive

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:38.507
and focus on What's really important,

00:13:38.507 --> 00:13:40.033
and in my case that's school.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:44.210
And it's also the work that
March for Our Lives does,

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.410
that doesn't get on the
news, focusing on mutual aid,

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:48.510
focusing on, the different aspects

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.960
of racial and economic justice

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:52.578
that plays into ending gun violence,

00:13:52.578 --> 00:13:54.710
and stopping people from
wanting to pick up a gun

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.560
in the first place, instead
of just talking about

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:57.610
how they get the gun.

00:14:00.058 --> 00:14:02.290
We have to have a holistic
solution here that we talk about.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:04.440
And those conversations,
unfortunately are things

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.420
that are great to fit in sound bites,

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:09.140
are great to put on the
news for a lot of people.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:11.089
'Cause they don't wanna talk
about the lack of resources

00:14:11.089 --> 00:14:13.101
that causes the community to,

00:14:13.101 --> 00:14:14.851
you know, that results in violence,

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.720
and historic, and systemic injustice.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.240
But that's the work that we're doing

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:23.150
and it hasn't been an easy transition,

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:26.650
but I've just realized, I
have to take care of myself,

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:29.788
and focus on what's ahead
with school, and yeah.

00:14:29.788 --> 00:14:30.621
I've been working on

00:14:30.621 --> 00:14:31.870
a couple of cool projects at school too.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.590
There's a public opinion polling project

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:36.810
that I worked on this semester for youth.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:38.320
So yeah, that was cool.

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:39.508
- Yeah, I mean a lot of ways,

00:14:39.508 --> 00:14:41.360
it sounds like you're letting yourself

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:42.910
ride that tension too.

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:46.290
'Cause there's so much
work that has to be done.

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:48.290
I mean, even as I'm listening
to you talking you talking,

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:50.618
I'm sure it was probably hits you too.

00:14:50.618 --> 00:14:52.040
Like, I mean that's a amount of work

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.730
that you're talking about
and that's not something

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:57.360
that anyone would be able to
do if they're spread too thin.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:58.940
But at the same time-
- Great,

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:00.440
considering what they can do themselves.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:01.750
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:03.100
I mean, would you say that you all have,

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:05.950
as a group have people bought
into this idea of self-care,

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:08.777
or has it been certain pockets
of people, like how has that?

00:15:08.777 --> 00:15:10.840
'Cause I'm just thinking about, you know,

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.870
I'm imagining what the vibe
was like when you all started,

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:13.980
how much energy there must've been,

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.920
as you all were touring
across the country.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:17.840
But I'm curious if that shift

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:20.110
is kinda pervaded, commercialized,

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:22.530
or do you think it's in pockets?

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:25.430
- I think it's in pockets,
I think it's more like...

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:26.783
I think a lot of the time,
especially, the young people

00:15:26.783 --> 00:15:29.480
that were involved in the
beginning, myself included,

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.200
but especially, for
some of the other people

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.840
that weren't as privileged as
me to have the power of being

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.650
not only being a guy, but also
being a white straight guy

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.000
in the first place like, inherently,

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.330
it's gonna be a lot easier for
me to exist in these spaces

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:44.480
than a lot of other
co-founders that were women,

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.723
or non-binary or were LGBTQ, or whatever.

00:15:49.348 --> 00:15:52.250
And for a lot of them they understand

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:54.070
they just got exhausted
and weren't accepting

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:58.450
of the space that wasn't, you know,

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:00.210
which is really difficult
for them to function in,

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:04.200
because young people are
important with HR experience,

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.060
unfortunately.

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:07.500
And in a youth-led organization I'll say

00:16:09.586 --> 00:16:13.460
the reason why March for Our
Lives has been successful

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:15.030
is because it is youth-led,

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:17.300
but it's also that very
same youth-led energy

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:19.010
that can also end the organization

00:16:19.010 --> 00:16:22.840
because it's the combination
of a lot of energy

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:27.840
and kind of adhocracy paired
with a lack of experience,

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:30.320
to be honest, it can be really damaging.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:32.553
So a lot of the, one
of the co-founders of,

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:35.341
taken a step back, which I think is fine,

00:16:35.341 --> 00:16:36.980
I think we see that
throughout these movements.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:38.540
There are people that take years off

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:40.240
at times and step back in.

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:42.490
And honestly, even if
some of the co-founders

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:45.010
don't ever wanna do this work again,

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:47.710
they've done more than
99.9% of people will ever do

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:48.750
in their lives around this issue.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.820
So I'm forever thankful for that.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.097
And I can't blame them,

00:16:52.097 --> 00:16:55.000
'cause ultimately, I want them alive.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.370
That's what it comes down to,
'cause this work is really,

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:01.340
it's really hard for these young people.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:06.130
So I think a lot of
like myself were forced

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:10.650
to learn about self care
by, to use a metaphor,

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.770
kind of like, you can tell
someone to not put their hand

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:16.970
on a hot stove a lot of times,

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:18.540
but sometimes it takes getting burned

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:20.980
to really realize the
importance of not doing that,

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:22.390
and I think that may be the case,

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:23.930
no, I don't mean to generalize,

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:26.170
but I think it as the case
with a lot of young people.

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:27.547
Especially, traumatized young people

00:17:27.547 --> 00:17:30.083
that care so much
understandably about this issue.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.190
But yeah, so a lot of the co-founders

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:35.810
have taken a step back
to focus on the arts,

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:38.360
focus on theater, focus on college,

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:39.320
which is totally valid,

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:42.180
and I'm never gonna blame them for that.

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.360
But I think as an organization,
we've gotten a lot better

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:47.150
at focusing on that self-care aspect.

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:48.400
We do a lot more workshops

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:50.863
and trainings around it and stuff now.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:53.900
But unfortunately, when it is young people

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:56.620
there is some element of
needing to learn it the hard way

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:57.570
at least in my case.

00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:59.123
'Cause I can't tell you how
many times I was told to,

00:17:59.123 --> 00:18:01.420
you know, "Make sure you're
taking care of yourself."

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:04.050
And I didn't understand,
I always heard that,

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:05.860
but I didn't understand
what that looked like for me

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:07.193
until I really sat down,

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.060
and started to kind of get reconnected

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:13.763
with my emotions, and my
experiences, and stuff.

00:18:13.763 --> 00:18:16.343
Because I think, especially with guys,

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:19.560
I know that I had a
tendency in the beginning

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.470
to just shut out all
the emotions and trauma,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:24.430
secondhand trauma that I
was hearing about every day

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:26.320
of people having their loved ones shot,

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:28.700
having even their pets
shot in front of them,

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:29.533
horrible stuff.

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:33.023
And just like kind of
like blocking it out,

00:18:33.023 --> 00:18:33.856
which I thought was the best
we way of dealing with it

00:18:34.692 --> 00:18:35.830
but it actually just piles up.

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:37.610
And I actually found myself,

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:42.610
I thought the PTSD and
the trauma and stuff

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:46.210
from activism, from obviously,
the shooting and stuff,

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:48.110
was just something that would make me sad,

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:48.943
and that's how I would know.

00:18:48.943 --> 00:18:51.210
But I didn't realize it just made me numb,

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:53.410
I didn't feel anything
for the longest time,

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:55.660
which was really concerning.

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.920
It got to the point
where every conversation

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.287
I heard was about gun violence.

00:19:00.287 --> 00:19:03.460
'Cause I was talking so
little to any of my friends

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:06.360
about anything else, 'cause
the work just became our life.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:11.080
And what it took for me
was really sitting down

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:12.660
and journaling every
day to get back in touch

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:16.430
with my emotions and to process
what that had gone through

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:18.630
in the past year, going
to therapy and stuff

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:21.013
to understand that those emotions

00:19:21.013 --> 00:19:24.350
are things that I have to
cope with as anyone else does,

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:26.390
and it's not just something
that you can just block out.

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:29.250
So that's been a lot of my journey around

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:31.940
the self-care aspect and
the co-founders as well,

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:36.680
and other students, but I'd be
lying if I said it was easy,

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:40.703
and there weren't people that are hurt,

00:19:40.703 --> 00:19:44.060
not necessarily by any fault
of their own, but just by,

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.520
you know, we're trying to do
the work of a government here,

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.520
essentially, because our
government is not doing this work.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.006
And that's not what we're supposed to do.

00:19:51.006 --> 00:19:52.050
We're supposed to have elected them,

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:53.410
we're supposed to be college students,

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:54.760
or high school students.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:55.700
We have elected officials

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:57.250
that are supposed to
represent us for a reason,

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:59.700
and it's exactly so we
shouldn't have to do this.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:03.200
- Yeah, that's so that's so important.

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:05.970
I mean, you've hit on so many
important pieces there too,

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:07.908
but I mean, I think I can just...

00:20:07.908 --> 00:20:09.290
It seems like you've really
gotten into a good place

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:11.060
of just realizing a lot of these things.

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:12.870
I mean, kind of the self-reflection,

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:14.440
even as you're talking
about the journaling,

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:16.220
that you're all doing more
work than you should be doing,

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.120
and just all the weight
that comes with that.

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:19.590
'Cause even as you were talking about,

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:21.430
all the families that
you've interacted with

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:22.650
across the country,

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:24.180
I can meet in my mind
what's going to like,

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.120
I mean, all those
conversations are weighty,

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.870
and in some ways I feel
like you must have,

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:31.920
even if he didn't know,
been carrying all that.

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:33.557
'Cause those stories you hear
them over and over again,

00:20:33.557 --> 00:20:35.520
but they're gonna penetrate at some point.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:36.990
I mean, it seems like a
lot of the ways you're able

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:40.780
to kinda channel that energy
into the increased activism,

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:42.030
then still you have your own story,

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:44.570
you have all these stories
that you're carrying with you,

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:46.740
and so really having a
place to process that

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:48.650
I think is really important.

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:50.310
It sounds like that's
been kind of a weekly,

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:51.880
daily process for you as of late,

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:54.530
just making sure that you
have the space for that,

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:55.740
which I think is critical,

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:58.067
but not always easy to acknowledge

00:20:58.067 --> 00:21:00.000
in the heat of the moment, in the sense,

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.160
when you're in the middle of the work.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:04.203
- Yeah, I think it goes,

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:09.010
it's all so important to deal
with that self care aspect,

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:12.723
because I have seen so
many times how in activism,

00:21:12.723 --> 00:21:16.360
I would be lying, if I said
it wasn't traumatizing work

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:18.030
for a lot of young
people, including myself,

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:21.070
like, getting called
by people with AR-15s,

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:23.410
getting screamed at by them,
having your house swatted,

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:25.410
death threats that I get,

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:28.230
but also a lot of kids that
work in the organization

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:29.740
and in the gun violence
prevention movement,

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:33.517
and people to in general get that trauma,

00:21:33.517 --> 00:21:36.870
and that exhaustion can end
up turning us on each other.

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:38.270
'Cause we start to think the reason

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:39.410
why we're not being successful

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:41.150
is because of people
that we're working with,

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:43.373
when in reality, it's because, you know,

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:46.640
you can say activism isn't easy,

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:50.460
or social change isn't easy,
but it is really, really hard.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:54.710
And it may not necessarily
be because of anybody's fault

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:57.090
that you work with, but you
can start to turn on each other

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:58.750
when you're not taking care of yourselves,

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:00.550
which I think is a major problem that,

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:05.550
not just the peace advocacy space has,

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:09.690
but also a problem that social
movements have in general,

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.663
because the work that we do
really is incredibly important.

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:15.277
But when we're not
taking care of ourselves,

00:22:15.277 --> 00:22:17.170
we can have a tendency
to turn on each other,

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:19.790
and get really negative and act
horribly towards each other.

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:21.950
And if you don't select times,

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:24.330
on a regular basis to really sit down

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:26.270
and talk those things out, it piles up,

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:28.000
and it really starts hurting people.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:29.350
- Yeah, yeah, I heard,

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:30.373
so important that you all
are paying attention to that.

00:22:30.373 --> 00:22:31.973
So I mean, even, all the things

00:22:31.973 --> 00:22:34.340
that you all have been through,
I mean, just in general,

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:36.289
when people have gone through
that as you know, I mean,

00:22:36.289 --> 00:22:39.660
it has an effect on
people's day-to-day lives,

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.630
just in terms of like,
schoolwork in general,

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:43.210
can they still focus as well?

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:45.450
Then you add on all the
activism work that you're doing,

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:47.150
which has a whole nother heightened level

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:51.800
of importance, intention,
and just weight to it,

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:52.990
and you put all that together.

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:57.120
So I think, yeah, that's
been at the forefront,

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:59.000
recently in other ways.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:00.730
I was thinking back to
one of the interviews

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:02.350
you and your sister did to, you know,

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:03.930
one of the things you talked
about reminded me of that.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:05.100
Just when you all were talking about

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:08.702
how the activism was
your therapy, in a sense.

00:23:08.702 --> 00:23:10.420
And it seems like what
you've been talking about

00:23:10.420 --> 00:23:11.970
ways that you're able
to kind of channel that

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:13.280
and use that as a way to cope.

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:16.700
Do you think that was effective
and helpful in some ways,

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:17.870
I mean, you've kind of
talked about the ways

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:19.260
where some things were ignored,

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:22.020
but you didn't pay as much
attention necessarily,

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:25.040
to some of the self-care
as you could have.

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:27.530
But what would you say
are some of the positives

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:29.990
of what you're doing and the activism,

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:32.260
the how that affected you all as people?

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:33.840
- I mean, I actually think sometimes

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:36.810
that like massive marches
and demonstrations,

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:41.000
even if not directly
politically impactful,

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.683
or something like that, I think,

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:47.150
it can actually be really
beneficial in some ways

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:48.660
because people realize they're not alone.

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:51.130
And I think that's been
one of the biggest benefits

00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:54.720
that we've seen a lot of social
movements from, you know,

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.680
feminist movements historically speaking,

00:23:56.680 --> 00:24:00.610
to civil rights, to to
peace, to anti-war movements,

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:03.010
in some ways they're almost like massive,

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:06.270
it's certainly not
certified as group therapy,

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:08.700
but in some ways it's kind of
like a massive venting session

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.780
that I think actually can be
really helpful for people.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:16.780
I think the issue comes when
those individuals continue

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:23.460
to focus so much on
that and sacrifice stuff

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:25.690
to try to end things that
are causing this trauma

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:27.770
that they end up throwing
away, the things that they love

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:30.120
and the people that they
love, not throwing them away.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:31.270
Throwing them away is too hard of a word,

00:24:31.270 --> 00:24:34.043
but not prioritizing them
just as much as the work.

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:38.130
And that ends up making
them feel really resentful,

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:38.963
towards the movement,

00:24:38.963 --> 00:24:40.090
towards the people that they work with,

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:41.550
because they feel like
they've sacrificed so much,

00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:42.970
and there's been so little progress.

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:45.910
Which honestly, there's
been a lot of progress

00:24:45.910 --> 00:24:47.870
in terms of the historical perspective

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:49.160
in gun violence prevention,

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:52.160
but on the scale that we needed at,

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:54.163
it's been very little, unfortunately.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:59.330
But I think, the activism
in some ways done properly

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:00.760
can be a form of therapy,

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:05.750
but done in a way that causes
harm, or done in a way that,

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:06.880
where you're not taking care of yourself

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:09.313
can be a form of trauma.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:12.080
When you're throwing away
the things that you love,

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.520
and just working on it every single day,

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:18.233
and there's no end in sight
of when it's gonna stop.

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:25.200
So yeah, that would be my
main way of thinking about it.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:27.390
- Yeah, and I think that's
a really good point.

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:30.400
I mean, the group therapy
piece in particular,

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:31.850
I mean, I think, there's a lot to that.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:34.220
'Cause there's that
common lived experience

00:25:34.220 --> 00:25:36.060
that you all have, so that
you can support each other,

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:39.920
and talking to someone who understands it.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:41.900
- Yeah, and there's a
lot of power in that,

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:44.540
because we feel like we're
surrounded, you know,

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:46.040
when you're around 800,000 people,

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:48.057
even if not all of them have
been affected by gun violence,

00:25:48.057 --> 00:25:51.220
a lot of them have been.

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:56.220
Like at the March, for example,
those people see each other,

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:57.650
and they see that they're not alone.

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:00.700
And I actually think that's a
huge amount of value in that.

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:02.940
Especially, with like the power of the NRA

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:06.330
and the people that
choose to try to advocate

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:09.030
for the rights of inanimate
objects over human beings,

00:26:10.833 --> 00:26:14.730
they're so loud, and they
have such strong connection

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:16.260
to each other than the people

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:19.280
that are actually affected
mostly by those actions

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:21.530
that want people and kids to be protected

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:23.390
not weapons like an AR-15.

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:25.550
If you're alone, even though
we're actually in the majority

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.963
if you do a poll, the country in general,

00:26:29.554 --> 00:26:33.510
that solidarity and friendship,
and community peace,

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:37.000
is extremely powerful
because it's that community

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:38.600
that enables social movements,

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:41.080
I believe in the longterm to succeed,

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:44.467
because at the core of
social movement power

00:26:44.467 --> 00:26:47.870
and the core political
power isn't politics at all,

00:26:47.870 --> 00:26:50.760
it's culture, it's
music, it's storytelling.

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:52.880
And it's only in those community spaces

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:56.920
when we're around each other,
that we're able to show love

00:26:56.920 --> 00:27:00.070
and appreciation for each
other and care for each other,

00:27:00.070 --> 00:27:02.360
even as unfortunately, no actions taken,

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:05.023
or even retroactive actions taken.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:11.000
We're able to be there
at kind of as the bedrock

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:13.100
and endurance that is necessary

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:14.510
for the sustainability of the movement.

00:27:14.510 --> 00:27:17.000
But when that community is only working

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:19.982
solely on the issue itself
and not really hanging out

00:27:19.982 --> 00:27:22.973
and talking with each other,
that's when things come in.

00:27:22.973 --> 00:27:25.270
And that's actually one thing
I realized, and did research,

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:26.140
that I did this semester,

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:27.760
it wasn't necessarily very scientific,

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:30.270
but it was just for a
history class of mine.

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:34.120
I was interviewing different
civil rights activists

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:36.730
that went to Harvard and worked
on like the freedom rights,

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:38.430
for example.
- Nice (indistinct).

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:43.043
- And one of them talked about how,

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:46.730
I won't say his name for privacy reasons,

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:49.300
but one of them talked about how,

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:51.790
when it was like ,I
think it was like 1963,

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:55.120
or something like that,
he went down to the south

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:56.600
to work on the freedom rights,

00:27:56.600 --> 00:27:58.320
and he was meeting up with
like some young people

00:27:58.320 --> 00:27:59.880
from the student non-violent
coordinating committee,

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:02.350
and everything, and he walked in the room

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:04.290
as a sophomore at Harvard, I think,

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:07.410
and he was expecting a ton of students

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:09.070
to be strategizing on a chalkboard

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:10.030
and like drawing all this stuff

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:11.620
out talking about policy and everything.

00:28:11.620 --> 00:28:14.932
But he walked in and these
young civil rights activists

00:28:14.932 --> 00:28:17.720
from all around the south, were
actually having a preach off

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:21.000
seeing who could imitate
MLK the best, and dancing,

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.700
and playing guitar, and singing together.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:25.447
And one thing you told me is like,

00:28:25.447 --> 00:28:28.000
"How could you see that and
not wanna be part of that?"

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:30.360
I think it's that community aspect

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:32.640
hanging out with each other, that music,

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:34.573
that really builds
successful social movements

00:28:34.573 --> 00:28:36.270
in the long run.

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:39.060
Because ultimately,
leaders in social movements

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:41.110
can have their own
impacts in different ways,

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:44.710
but the real power in
those, it's in the culture,

00:28:44.710 --> 00:28:49.120
and it's in the community,
because, some movements are ones

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:52.750
where everyone are leaders
in the first place,

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:53.980
where everybody is able to lead themselves

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:54.933
and take care of each other.

00:28:54.933 --> 00:28:58.030
And when we're not doing that,
when we're not having fun,

00:28:58.030 --> 00:28:59.050
it's not sustainable.

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:01.070
So that's been a big focus of myself

00:29:01.070 --> 00:29:04.180
and the organization over
the past years figuring out

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:06.220
how can still cope with COVID,

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:07.520
but how can we celebrate our wins,

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:10.890
how can we take time to hang
out and do things not related

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:13.090
to incredibly exhausting
and traumatized work,

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:15.750
like trying to stop
people from getting shot.

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:16.930
- Yeah, it's so important.

00:29:16.930 --> 00:29:18.790
I mean, the research backs us up too,

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:19.687
in terms of the psychology research

00:29:19.687 --> 00:29:21.780
that's been done looking
at the power of community

00:29:21.780 --> 00:29:23.740
even in terms of the
neuroscience research,

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:25.560
looking at what being a
community does to the brain

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:27.720
and how it helps with just
moving through stresses

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:29.051
and resilience, I think, I mean.

00:29:29.051 --> 00:29:29.884
- Yeah, you're right.

00:29:29.884 --> 00:29:32.470
- Everything you're saying
is spot on in a lot of ways.

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:33.877
How would you say you're cultivating that?

00:29:33.877 --> 00:29:35.500
'Cause it seems like
there's two pieces there.

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:37.700
There's both kinds of
self-care, the daily self-care,

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:40.420
but then also making sure
there's the community self care,

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:44.123
making sure that those
are ingrained in a way.

00:29:45.170 --> 00:29:48.500
- So I think one thing that
we are trying to do kind of,

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.270
is trying to develop some kind of system

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:53.530
to deal with the inevitable grievances

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:55.880
that come up between the
organizers and leaders

00:29:57.190 --> 00:30:00.400
on an annual basis where
we just talk it out,

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:03.493
we talk about and move on.

00:30:06.119 --> 00:30:07.354
But it's also...

00:30:07.354 --> 00:30:09.354
The way that I practice it personally is

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:15.490
I just call up friends
randomly that I've worked with,

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:17.410
which, you know, some people
don't like being called,

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:18.590
or whatever, but I'll text them,

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:21.077
or I just like talking to them and stuff.

00:30:21.077 --> 00:30:23.299
And I ask them, I basically,

00:30:23.299 --> 00:30:25.277
at some point I checked in with them

00:30:25.277 --> 00:30:26.960
and I just talked to him casually.

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:30.120
But really, it's a good
way of checking myself

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:31.390
to make sure I'm taking care of myself,

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:34.410
but it's a good way of
having accountability

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:35.330
for making sure other people

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:36.700
are taking care of themselves well.

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:38.287
So I think if we look

00:30:38.287 --> 00:30:40.550
at the history of the civil
rights movement, unfortunately,

00:30:40.550 --> 00:30:43.270
because of understandably the
incredible amount of trauma

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:46.050
and stuff behind it,
there's a lot of people...

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:47.557
We hear a lot about like
the successes of the leaders

00:30:47.557 --> 00:30:49.183
and stuff in different everyday people

00:30:49.183 --> 00:30:51.710
that have participated, where
we don't hear a lot about

00:30:51.710 --> 00:30:54.030
is even, the people that ended up

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:58.610
abusing various substances, and
stuff because of the trauma,

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:00.950
and not being able to
deal with it and stuff.

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:03.650
And, I don't know how much of that you can

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:05.400
when it's that level of trauma,

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:06.233
I don't know how much of that

00:31:06.233 --> 00:31:09.080
you can actually just deal
with through community.

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:10.150
But I think that's
something that we're trying

00:31:10.150 --> 00:31:11.757
to develop in the organization.

00:31:11.757 --> 00:31:13.900
But just personally, I
just call friends randomly

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:15.997
a lot of the time and checking
in with them and I'm like,

00:31:15.997 --> 00:31:17.940
"Hey, so what are you doing
to take care of yourself?

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:21.630
What are you doing that
you love, that you enjoy?

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:26.190
And, how are you making sure
that you're not underwater,

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:27.568
basically, and taking on too much stuff

00:31:27.568 --> 00:31:31.110
because we need people to
be here for decades to come,

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:34.910
possibly, and not just the next
two years, but the next 20,

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:37.500
or even longer, hopefully not,

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:39.170
but we we need to be prepared for that.

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:41.220
So I've found that that's a good way

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:43.085
of checking in with people because,

00:31:43.085 --> 00:31:47.680
I think there's a bad
tendency that I still have

00:31:47.680 --> 00:31:49.430
but not nearly as bad as I used to,

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:50.680
and I know a lot of other people

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:52.210
who've had in the organization in the past

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:54.830
where we'd only talk to each
other in moments of crisis,

00:31:54.830 --> 00:31:56.895
or when you need to do like mobilize.

00:31:56.895 --> 00:31:57.888
And when you have that

00:31:57.888 --> 00:32:00.540
I think there comes to be an
association with each other

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:03.653
of really negative
feelings and grievances,

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:06.870
if you're only mobilizing after,

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:09.280
say another shooting happens
or something like that,

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:10.450
it's not healthy.

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:13.370
And by focusing on talking to each other

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:17.530
in a non-crisis times, it's
a good way of realizing

00:32:17.530 --> 00:32:19.307
that we're on the same side here,

00:32:19.307 --> 00:32:22.220
and it helps reduce those
like interpersonal conflicts,

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:24.060
and make sure that we're
taking care of ourselves,

00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:27.630
which further helps to reduce
those interpersonal conflicts.

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:29.280
And it's just a good way
of forming relationships,

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:31.870
'cause ultimately, at
that core political hours,

00:32:31.870 --> 00:32:34.930
I talked about in the past
here, I think it's culture,

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:37.720
but it's also that
community and the fabric,

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:39.850
like the the strings that
make up that community,

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:41.660
and we use it together are relationships.

00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:43.590
And they're not relationships
that are just made

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:45.800
because we're only talking about work,

00:32:45.800 --> 00:32:47.450
they're made because you
understand what's going on

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:50.410
with each other, what's going
on with each other's families,

00:32:50.410 --> 00:32:52.940
what's going on with each
other's significant others,

00:32:52.940 --> 00:32:55.380
or whatever, and going on personally,

00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:56.600
and you're there to support them,

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:57.969
even if it's not work-related.

00:32:57.969 --> 00:33:02.969
I think those are the
best type of activist,

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:05.890
community relationships are the ones

00:33:05.890 --> 00:33:08.877
where they're not at their
core, they're not just to run,

00:33:08.877 --> 00:33:10.430
"Okay, we need to do X thing."

00:33:10.430 --> 00:33:12.640
It's around actually knowing
what's going on with each other

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:14.840
and understanding why we do this work,

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:15.950
and each other's motives,

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:18.230
and the challenges that
each of us uniquely face.

00:33:18.230 --> 00:33:19.640
- Yeah, that's so true,
'cause then you have

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:20.473
that foundation too, like you said,

00:33:20.473 --> 00:33:22.936
it's not just focused on the mobilizing,

00:33:22.936 --> 00:33:25.224
or the trauma of this kinda
built throughout that.

00:33:25.224 --> 00:33:27.620
Again, the research
would back that up too,

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:30.830
and there's so many
important pieces there.

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:33.320
I'm curious if you feel like
there's enough resources

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:35.210
in place to, 'cause I mean, one of the...

00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:36.043
I don't do trauma work specifically,

00:33:36.043 --> 00:33:39.120
but I know one of the things
that comes up quite a bit

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:41.110
is making sure that people really know

00:33:41.110 --> 00:33:43.281
how to help other people
walk through that.

00:33:43.281 --> 00:33:44.610
'Cause you get a lot of instances

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:47.620
where people don't know
how to handle that.

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:49.110
They have good intentions
but they're actually

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:50.970
just end up retraumatizing people.

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:53.400
I mean, we talked about
that before you gone,

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:56.280
it just even in terms of
some of the interviews

00:33:56.280 --> 00:33:57.880
that you've done, and
how it kinda gets back

00:33:57.880 --> 00:33:59.920
to that same thing over and over again.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.900
So I wonder if you all have
even thought about that

00:34:01.900 --> 00:34:03.090
in terms of the movement,

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:05.460
like do you feel like
there are more resources,

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:07.899
and obviously that's a
challenge if resources are thin.

00:34:07.899 --> 00:34:09.870
Furthermore, resources
that should be in place

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.610
to help people actually
process that trauma

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:16.120
as they're engaged in the work
so that it's not, you know,

00:34:16.120 --> 00:34:17.520
going into a place that just making it

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:19.520
that much more difficult,
even with good intentions,

00:34:19.520 --> 00:34:21.260
but unintentionally.

00:34:21.260 --> 00:34:23.127
- Right, yeah, I mean, I think that

00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:26.290
we certainly have more resources
than we did in the past,

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:27.683
but it's not nearly enough.

00:34:29.470 --> 00:34:31.422
I'm just talking about
the movement as a whole,

00:34:31.422 --> 00:34:33.763
which includes March for Our Lives.

00:34:34.740 --> 00:34:37.970
I think one of the difficulties is that

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:41.730
we're so focused on spending the money

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:43.695
on the work that needs to be done.

00:34:43.695 --> 00:34:46.650
It can seem hard for us to justify it,

00:34:46.650 --> 00:34:48.557
like, if we need to pay for a therapist,

00:34:48.557 --> 00:34:51.943
or some kind of community
counselor or something.

00:34:52.820 --> 00:34:53.930
Even though in my opinion,

00:34:53.930 --> 00:34:56.200
like resources are severely lacking,

00:34:56.200 --> 00:34:59.520
but honestly, the biggest
obstacle besides the NRA

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:02.470
to us actually being successful
I would say is our trauma,

00:35:02.470 --> 00:35:04.440
and the divisions that it creates.

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:09.440
And It can be hard to justify to donors,

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:12.523
or people that just give a
small amount or whatever.

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:17.210
Spending a couple $1000, for example,

00:35:17.210 --> 00:35:19.650
on helping reduce community trauma,

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:21.790
and all these things that sound very big,

00:35:21.790 --> 00:35:24.089
but really are important.
- Yeah, they are.

00:35:24.089 --> 00:35:25.830
- And managing better relationships

00:35:25.830 --> 00:35:27.310
and how to do those trainings,

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:30.060
instead of spending that
money on registering

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:31.080
a couple 100 new voters,

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:33.060
or a couple 1000 new voters or whatever.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:35.661
Even though the only reason why we...

00:35:35.661 --> 00:35:37.564
The overall return on investment,

00:35:37.564 --> 00:35:39.942
if that's really what
we're talking about here

00:35:39.942 --> 00:35:43.010
is significantly more. If
we're able to do more work,

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:45.460
and get more volunteers and
develop a stronger community,

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:47.840
if we're able to deal
successfully with that trauma.

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:50.330
But I would say, that's one
of the difficult things,

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:53.233
is honestly, if I could I would,

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:56.960
if we had the money to do it,
and people were more accepting

00:35:56.960 --> 00:35:59.630
of understanding the importance of that,

00:35:59.630 --> 00:36:04.630
I would love to hire way more
HR people and not just HR,

00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:08.320
but I don't know what term to come up with

00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:10.540
that doesn't sound incredibly
vague, and like BS.

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:12.700
But I guess like people

00:36:12.700 --> 00:36:14.640
that really help manage community wellness

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:17.700
and like developing proper protocols

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:22.410
between how to train people,
especially in the past year,

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:25.550
since George Floyd and
everything like talking...

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:26.840
It's difficult when there's kids

00:36:26.840 --> 00:36:28.850
from all different communities,
all different races,

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:31.600
that oftentimes, you never
really worked together

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:32.774
in a social justice...

00:36:32.774 --> 00:36:35.053
Remember, they might've gone
to school together, and stuff.

00:36:36.059 --> 00:36:38.500
But the dynamics of
racial identity and trauma

00:36:38.500 --> 00:36:41.230
in the United States, and talking about it

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:44.620
is something that a lot of,
to be completely honest,

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:47.418
just white kids do not understand at all.

00:36:47.418 --> 00:36:49.970
It's something that I'm
still learning about,

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:51.437
if I'm being completely honest,

00:36:51.437 --> 00:36:52.940
and I'm always trying to get better at it,

00:36:52.940 --> 00:36:55.530
but it's not something we're born knowing.

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:57.350
Especially, and we
certainly don't learn it

00:36:57.350 --> 00:36:58.650
from society or the media.

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:02.490
So, yeah, I don't think
we have necessarily

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:04.790
at all enough resources
to deal with that trauma,

00:37:04.790 --> 00:37:08.951
unfortunately, 'cause it's hard
to justify, but it's needed.

00:37:08.951 --> 00:37:10.950
And we have done some things around it.

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:14.903
We try to have like occasional, somewhat,

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:18.660
we try to give people
time off, have retreats,

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:19.493
and we try to be really affirming

00:37:19.493 --> 00:37:22.000
of when people need to go on vacation,

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:25.513
and really celebrate that,
because like, this work is hard,

00:37:25.513 --> 00:37:27.290
it's really hard, and our issue is not

00:37:27.290 --> 00:37:28.410
that people don't wanna do the work,

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:30.290
it's that they wanna do too much.

00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:33.530
- Yup, (laughs) I mean,
yeah, in a lot of ways,

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:35.600
you're talking about a cultural shift too.

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:37.830
Because some of those
resources are needed,

00:37:37.830 --> 00:37:39.730
and I agree with you
that makes things better

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:43.560
in the long run, but it
doesn't look as shiny

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:45.770
if you're trying to raise them like,

00:37:45.770 --> 00:37:48.690
it doesn't look as much of an investment,

00:37:48.690 --> 00:37:50.461
'cause you can't see
that outcome right away.

00:37:50.461 --> 00:37:51.294
- And they're telling it
- But unfortunately,

00:37:51.294 --> 00:37:52.127
- on the news.
- Like you said, you can see

00:37:52.127 --> 00:37:54.280
what happens when it's not addressed,

00:37:54.280 --> 00:37:56.183
but-
- Right, yeah.

00:37:57.094 --> 00:38:00.920
You know, unfortunately a lot of the work

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:02.260
that needs to be done in the organization

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:04.470
and in the social justice space

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:09.420
is not to end gun violence
or address the things

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:10.960
that play into gun violence;
like racial justice,

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:13.247
or environmental injustice,
or economic injustice,

00:38:13.247 --> 00:38:14.690
are not things that necessarily get

00:38:14.690 --> 00:38:15.943
on the news super easily.

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:19.970
For example, March this
year committed to a...

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:21.270
We have a half million dollar commitment

00:38:21.270 --> 00:38:23.660
to grants and focus on
mutual aid, you know;

00:38:23.660 --> 00:38:26.370
providing food, clothing,
whatever communities need,

00:38:26.370 --> 00:38:28.300
and going specifically to the community,

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:29.133
the black and brown communities

00:38:29.133 --> 00:38:30.966
that are most impacted by gun violence

00:38:30.966 --> 00:38:34.850
asking them how we can be supportive

00:38:34.850 --> 00:38:37.904
of the community
organizations like Life Camp,

00:38:37.904 --> 00:38:39.604
I'm wearing their shirt right now,

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:42.060
doing the work on the ground,

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:43.820
stopping kids from shooting each other,

00:38:43.820 --> 00:38:45.960
or everyday people from
shooting each other.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:46.987
Instead of going to them and saying,

00:38:46.987 --> 00:38:47.820
"This is what we're gonna do for you:

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:50.710
we're gonna throw $10,000 at you,

00:38:50.710 --> 00:38:51.630
and you're just gonna be happy.

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:54.670
And like we did our job,
we're equitable or whatever."

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:56.920
We're gonna really ask
'cause we understand,

00:38:56.920 --> 00:38:59.010
I don't know what goes on in,
for example, Jamaica, Queens,

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:02.620
where this shirt of LIFE
Camp that I've worked with,

00:39:02.620 --> 00:39:04.010
I don't know what goes on there.

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:06.200
Erica Ford, who's been
doing this work for 20 years

00:39:06.200 --> 00:39:08.360
in Jamaica, Queens and is stopping kids

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:10.800
from shooting each other every day.

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:12.220
She knows what needs to be done.

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:13.255
So we ask people like her,

00:39:13.255 --> 00:39:14.880
"what can we do to be supportive?"

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:17.270
But even though there's a big commitment,

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:18.750
which is a significant part of our budget,

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:20.020
we're not like the NRA,

00:39:20.020 --> 00:39:22.020
we don't have a $100,000,000 budget,

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:23.493
or whatever a year at all.

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:30.070
We get by, but it's still
nothing in comparison to that.

00:39:30.740 --> 00:39:31.910
Even though there's that commitment

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:34.190
and the work that comes out of it,

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:35.800
it doesn't get on the news.

00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:38.470
So it's hard because like
we know that that's the work

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:40.920
that needs to be done to actually be...

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:43.790
To help advance the movement forward,

00:39:43.790 --> 00:39:46.680
but it's also difficult
because we know that

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:48.070
a lot of that money in comparison

00:39:48.070 --> 00:39:52.160
to spending it on something
like, a big event or something,

00:39:52.160 --> 00:39:53.450
it's not gonna get on the news,

00:39:53.450 --> 00:39:55.540
but we know that that's the
work that needs to be done.

00:39:55.540 --> 00:39:57.290
I think that's actually
a really big problem

00:39:57.290 --> 00:39:58.811
with the nonprofit industrial complex,

00:39:58.811 --> 00:40:00.620
if I'm being completely honest,

00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:03.260
is that we have a system of incentives

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:05.670
that incentivize people to do
things that get on the news,

00:40:05.670 --> 00:40:08.600
even if they're not necessarily effective.

00:40:08.600 --> 00:40:10.240
I can't tell you the number of times

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:12.340
I've seen an organization spend six,

00:40:12.340 --> 00:40:13.650
even seven figures on things

00:40:13.650 --> 00:40:16.230
that could have been massively impactful,

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:19.283
for extremely under-resourced
community organizations,

00:40:20.413 --> 00:40:22.630
and have a huge impact,
but they choose instead

00:40:22.630 --> 00:40:25.990
to spend on something that's
gonna get on CNN, or whatever,

00:40:25.990 --> 00:40:28.280
even though it's not gonna
have that much of an impact

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:30.400
in comparison to where that
money actually really could go.

00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:33.777
So it's difficult, because
then we get questions like,

00:40:33.777 --> 00:40:35.300
"Oh what is March for Our Lives doing?"

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:36.470
It's like, "Well, we're focused

00:40:36.470 --> 00:40:37.570
on needs mutual aid programs.

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:38.493
We're focusing on talking about

00:40:38.493 --> 00:40:40.500
what we call arms supremacy."

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:43.920
Like the role of guns in
white supremacist circles

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:46.120
and actions and the
enforcement of white supremacy

00:40:46.120 --> 00:40:47.360
in the United States.

00:40:47.360 --> 00:40:48.620
we're talking about mental health,

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:50.742
but not in a way where it's like,

00:40:50.742 --> 00:40:53.190
"Let's talk about the mental health

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:54.360
and the shooter at my high school

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:55.580
and why you decided to do that."

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:58.080
And talk about that only
because he's a white kid.

00:40:59.890 --> 00:41:02.240
And we tend to stereotype
white man shooters

00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:03.490
as mentally ill because
we don't wanna talk

00:41:03.490 --> 00:41:06.370
about the fact that they're a
lot of the time, not always,

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:08.740
but a significant amount
of the time it's simply,

00:41:08.740 --> 00:41:13.740
I can't say simply, but it's
in large part if not entirely,

00:41:13.750 --> 00:41:15.103
because they're racist.

00:41:16.458 --> 00:41:19.320
The shooter at my high school
was racist and antisemitic.

00:41:19.320 --> 00:41:22.468
He was known to have drawn
things like swastikas on stuff.

00:41:22.468 --> 00:41:27.230
I don't see that racism and antisemitism

00:41:27.230 --> 00:41:28.573
as mental illness.

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:31.750
But it's difficult because
we wanna talk about

00:41:31.750 --> 00:41:33.426
mental health in regards to
the two-thirds gun deaths

00:41:33.426 --> 00:41:35.997
that are suicides and don't
get on the news, right?

00:41:35.997 --> 00:41:36.830
- Right.

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:38.430
- That's really what
needs to be addressed.

00:41:38.430 --> 00:41:40.540
If we wanna talk about
addressing total gun deaths

00:41:40.540 --> 00:41:43.020
we should be talking
two-thirds of the time

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:45.500
if not more about gun suicide.

00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:47.410
But because the mental health conversation

00:41:47.410 --> 00:41:50.690
is only around who a mass shooter is...

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:52.210
I cannot tell you I've had conversations

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:54.960
with poor kids that said
they were depressed.

00:41:54.960 --> 00:41:56.600
So they went through the school counselor.

00:41:56.600 --> 00:41:59.240
And the school counselor put
them on a list saying that

00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:01.860
they were possible mass shooter
because they were depressed

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:03.900
and they were worried
about hurting themselves.

00:42:03.900 --> 00:42:05.800
That is the effect that
the media is having

00:42:05.800 --> 00:42:07.017
on these conversations.

00:42:07.017 --> 00:42:09.520
And it's so hard because we can't...

00:42:10.980 --> 00:42:13.090
Those conversations
around armed supremacy,

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:16.180
around mental health but
around suicide prevention,

00:42:16.180 --> 00:42:18.317
and around mutual aid and racial justice

00:42:18.317 --> 00:42:21.290
are not easy things to
fit in a soundbite like,

00:42:21.290 --> 00:42:23.120
universal background checks,

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:27.270
which yes it can have an impact,
but ultimately, isn't it...

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:29.380
For a lot of cities that
have stronger gun laws,

00:42:29.380 --> 00:42:31.580
although, there's certainly
a lack of federal ones,

00:42:31.580 --> 00:42:34.513
a lot of these guns that
get there quite frankly,

00:42:35.390 --> 00:42:37.830
get there illegally because there's a lack

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:40.870
of like federal guidance around
interstate gun trafficking

00:42:40.870 --> 00:42:42.948
that could actually prevent it.

00:42:42.948 --> 00:42:45.620
It's extremely hard to
get a gun in Chicago,

00:42:45.620 --> 00:42:47.650
or it's not necessarily
as hard as it could be,

00:42:47.650 --> 00:42:49.580
but it's still hard nonetheless,

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:51.530
you can just drive 30 minutes to Indiana,

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:53.040
and get a gun and come back,

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:54.610
or some guy can go and buy 50 guns

00:42:54.610 --> 00:42:56.130
and sell them out of
the back of his truck,

00:42:56.130 --> 00:42:59.043
legally, a lot of the time,
on the Salter west side,

00:42:59.043 --> 00:43:01.290
and have them go out that way.

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:02.790
But what we need to address is,

00:43:02.790 --> 00:43:05.450
why do people on this
ultimate side feel the need

00:43:05.450 --> 00:43:06.740
to pick up a gun in the first place?

00:43:06.740 --> 00:43:08.803
It's because they don't have resources.

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:12.660
Parkland has significantly
weaker gun laws than Chicago

00:43:12.660 --> 00:43:15.550
than many other cities in the country.

00:43:15.550 --> 00:43:17.510
We don't have shootings on a daily basis.

00:43:17.510 --> 00:43:22.510
And it's not because we have
the most police officer,

00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:26.510
or the most stringent gun laws,

00:43:26.510 --> 00:43:28.560
it's because we had the most resources.

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:30.640
And having those conversations are not

00:43:30.640 --> 00:43:33.140
that those nuances are extremely hard

00:43:33.140 --> 00:43:35.363
to get across in a TV news interview.

00:43:35.363 --> 00:43:38.300
It doesn't fit into the
neatly package, trauma,

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:39.990
industrial media complex

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:42.340
that commodifies these mass shootings

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:43.641
where they have graphics,

00:43:43.641 --> 00:43:47.020
like it's some kinda
baseball game or something.

00:43:47.020 --> 00:43:50.040
Where it's like, "This is how
many people you know, die.

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:51.780
This is how it compares
to other mass shootings

00:43:51.780 --> 00:43:52.613
and things like that."

00:43:52.613 --> 00:43:54.308
Without any regard to
the fact that most people

00:43:54.308 --> 00:43:56.840
that are gonna die today from gun violence

00:43:56.840 --> 00:43:59.440
are self-inflicted gunshot wounds.

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:01.620
They are unintentional shooting,

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:02.790
and they're everyday acts

00:44:02.790 --> 00:44:05.823
of interpersonal gun violence
and domestic violence.

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:08.365
That's what needs to get
on the news but doesn't.

00:44:08.365 --> 00:44:12.669
So it's simple, because what
we need to do is over here,

00:44:12.669 --> 00:44:16.370
it's in one corner and we
know what we need to do,

00:44:16.370 --> 00:44:19.220
but what actually enables
people to get a good like,

00:44:19.220 --> 00:44:20.520
the nonprofit industrial complex

00:44:20.520 --> 00:44:21.850
to get a return on investment

00:44:21.850 --> 00:44:23.940
so they can continue doing the work

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:25.700
might be in a completely different space.

00:44:25.700 --> 00:44:27.253
And there's plenty of people that do that,

00:44:27.253 --> 00:44:29.327
and then we get people asking,
"Well, what are we doing?"

00:44:29.327 --> 00:44:32.090
And the answer is we're
doing more work than we have,

00:44:32.090 --> 00:44:33.760
and we're in the best
place the organization,

00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:36.350
in my opinion, has ever been
in terms of sustainability.

